{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792696298,"nonce":"ivP0GDW2CRXRDGYji6wpyGqdu2w=","title":"TypeScript: array bounds proof for cached lengths and bounded loops","message":"An index is accepted only when the frontend proves it is in range. The proof recognizes i \u003c xs.length (loop condition or branch) and, since 3497149 slice 1, loops starting from a non-negative integer expression such as i + 1. It does not recognize a cached length (const n = xs.length; for (let i = 0; i \u003c n; i++)) or a numeric parameter bound (i \u003c n with n a parameter). Both are everywhere in benchmark code, and the failure is \"array indexing without proven integer bounds\".\n\nNeeded: record that a binding holds the length of a specific array identity while that identity's length cannot change (no push/pop/length write between the alias and the use, no reassignment), and accept i \u003c alias as i \u003c xs.length. A parameter bound cannot be proven statically; decide whether a runtime check that traps, or an Optional element read, is acceptable under the exact-or-refused rule, or whether such loops stay unsupported.\n\nRelated: arrays.rs narrow_array, native_array_induction, FlowState.bounds.","files":null}]}